BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Clearly trivial misstatement threshold application
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Cascade Electronics, the auditor identifies a $200 error in office supply expense. Overall materiality is $500,000 and the clearly trivial threshold is $25,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should treat this misstatement by:', '["Accumulating it on the summary of uncorrected misstatements for governance review", "Communicating it individually to those charged with governance as a potential concern", "Disregarding it because it falls below the clearly trivial threshold", "Adjusting performance materiality downward to capture smaller errors"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.05, the auditor need not accumulate misstatements that are clearly trivial—amounts so small they could not individually or in aggregate be material. A $200 error is well below the $25,000 clearly trivial threshold and may be appropriately disregarded. Accumulating it on the summary (choice A) would be unnecessarily conservative when the amount is clearly inconsequential by any measure of size, nature, or circumstances.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Deficiency in design versus deficiency in operation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While reviewing controls at Juniper Financial Group, the auditor observes that the company has a policy requiring dual authorization for wire transfers over $10,000. However, the system allows single-user processing because the configuration was never updated. This situation best illustrates:', '["A deficiency in the design of the wire transfer control", "A deficiency in the operation of the wire transfer control", "A material weakness requiring immediate public disclosure", "An inherent limitation of the information system beyond management''s control"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A1, a deficiency in operation exists when a properly designed control does not operate as designed or when the person performing it lacks authority or competence. Here, the dual-authorization policy is properly designed, but the system was never configured to enforce it, meaning the control fails in operation. A design deficiency (choice A) would exist only if the control, even when operating as designed, could not prevent or detect misstatements.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Prior-period uncorrected misstatements affecting current year
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pinecrest Hospitality, the auditor notes $45,000 in uncorrected misstatements from the prior year that affect current-year opening balances. Overall materiality for the current year is $300,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Include the effect of prior-year uncorrected misstatements in the current-year evaluation of aggregate misstatements", "Ignore prior-year misstatements since they were previously evaluated and accepted", "Require management to restate the prior-year comparative financial statements before continuing", "Issue a qualified opinion solely based on the prior-year carry-forward amounts"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor must evaluate whether uncorrected misstatements from prior periods affect the current-period financial statements individually or in aggregate. The $45,000 carry-forward must be considered alongside current-year misstatements when determining whether the aggregate is material. Ignoring them (choice B) would be incorrect because prior-period misstatements can affect current-period balances and trends, potentially causing material misstatement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Iron curtain versus rollover approach to misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Summit Ridge Corporation has accumulated $180,000 in uncorrected income-inflating misstatements over three years, all originating in the earliest year. Current-year materiality is $200,000. Under the rollover approach, only $20,000 originated this year. When evaluating the aggregate effect under AU-C 450, the auditor should consider that:', '["The rollover approach alone is sufficient since current-year originations are immaterial", "The iron curtain approach alone should be used since it captures the full balance sheet effect", "Both the iron curtain and rollover approaches should inform the evaluation of whether the financial statements are materially misstated", "Neither approach is relevant because the misstatements were individually evaluated in prior years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A18, the auditor considers both quantitative and qualitative factors when evaluating uncorrected misstatements. The iron curtain approach (total $180,000 impact on current statements) and rollover approach ($20,000 current-year origination) each provide useful but incomplete perspectives. Relying solely on the rollover approach (choice A) could mask a near-material cumulative balance sheet error, while the iron curtain approach alone may overstate current-year audit risk.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Management bias in accounting estimates over multiple periods
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Northbridge Lending, the auditor observes that management consistently selects assumptions at the optimistic end of the reasonable range for the loan loss reserve, resulting in a $70,000 understatement each year. Individually, each year''s amount is below the $100,000 materiality threshold. Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor should:', '["Accept each year''s estimate since it falls within the reasonable range of outcomes", "Evaluate whether the pattern of bias indicates intentional misstatement and consider its cumulative effect on the financial statements", "Require management to use the midpoint of the acceptable range for all estimates going forward", "Report the bias directly to regulators as evidence of fraudulent financial reporting"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.11 and .A20, the auditor evaluates whether uncorrected misstatements reflect a possible bias by management. A consistent pattern of optimistic assumptions, even when each falls within the reasonable range, may indicate intentional bias that in aggregate causes material misstatement. Simply accepting each estimate individually (choice A) ignores the auditor''s obligation to evaluate cumulative effects and potential management bias across reporting periods.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Clearly trivial misstatement definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, what is a clearly trivial misstatement?', '["An amount so small it could not be material to the financial statements individually or in aggregate", "Any misstatement that falls below overall materiality for the engagement", "A misstatement that management has agreed to correct before year-end", "A misstatement caused by an unintentional error rather than fraud"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 450.05 defines clearly trivial as amounts that are clearly inconsequential, whether taken individually or in aggregate and whether judged by any criteria of size, nature, or circumstances. This threshold is set well below performance materiality, typically at 3-5% of overall materiality. A misstatement below overall materiality (choice B) is too broad—misstatements above clearly trivial but below materiality must still be accumulated and evaluated.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Restatement as strong indicator of material weakness
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'After Ridgewood Pharmaceuticals issues its audited financial statements, the company discovers an error requiring restatement of previously reported revenue by $2.3 million. The auditor is evaluating control deficiencies for the current-year audit. Under AU-C 265, a restatement of previously issued financial statements is:', '["A significant deficiency that must be communicated only to management for corrective action", "A strong indicator that a material weakness exists in internal control", "Relevant only if the restatement amount exceeds current-year overall materiality", "Outside the scope of the auditor''s current-year evaluation of internal control"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, a restatement to correct a material misstatement in previously issued financial statements is a strong indicator that a material weakness exists. The restatement demonstrates that controls failed to prevent or detect a material error before issuance. Classifying it as merely a significant deficiency (choice A) understates the severity, since the control failure allowed an actual material misstatement to pass through the financial reporting process undetected.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Qualitative factors in misstatement evaluation for related parties
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Brightwater Energy, the auditor identifies a $30,000 uncorrected misstatement in related-party disclosures. Overall materiality is $500,000. Although quantitatively immaterial, the misstatement obscures a significant transaction with the CEO''s family member. The auditor should:', '["Conclude the misstatement is immaterial since it is well below the materiality threshold", "Accept the misstatement after documenting only the quantitative assessment in the workpapers", "Evaluate whether the qualitative nature of the misstatement affects the fairness of the financial statements as a whole", "Automatically issue a qualified opinion for any related-party disclosure error"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.11 and .A19, the auditor considers both quantitative and qualitative factors when evaluating uncorrected misstatements. Misstatements affecting related-party disclosures may be qualitatively material even when quantitatively small, because they affect users'' understanding of related-party transactions. Dismissing it based solely on the dollar amount (choice A) ignores the qualitative significance that AU-C 450 requires the auditor to consider in the evaluation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Deficiency in design definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, what is a deficiency in the design of an internal control?', '["A control that exists but is not performed consistently by trained personnel", "A control that was effective in prior years but has since deteriorated over time", "A control that relies on information technology rather than manual review processes", "A control that is missing or improperly designed so the control objective would not be met even if it operated as intended"]'::jsonb, 3, 'AU-C 265.07 states a deficiency in design exists when a control necessary to meet a control objective is missing, or when an existing control is not properly designed so that even if it operates as designed, the control objective would not be met. A control that exists but is not performed consistently (choice A) describes a deficiency in operation, not design. The distinction matters for evaluating severity and remediation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Written representation regarding uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Meridian Health Systems has accumulated $85,000 in uncorrected misstatements during the audit. Overall materiality is $400,000. Before issuing the audit report, the auditor requests management to provide a written representation. Under AU-C 450, the representation regarding uncorrected misstatements should include:', '["Management''s agreement to correct all identified misstatements in the subsequent reporting period", "A statement from management that the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial individually and in aggregate to the financial statements as a whole", "Management''s assertion that no misstatements were identified during the course of the audit", "An acknowledgment that all identified misstatements have been recorded in the general ledger"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.14, the auditor requests a written representation from management that the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. A summary of the uncorrected items is included with or attached to the representation letter. Requiring correction next period (choice A) is not the standard requirement—management may legitimately decline to correct immaterial misstatements.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Misstatement impact on debt covenant compliance
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Cedar Valley Logistics, the auditor identifies $120,000 in uncorrected misstatements that would cause the company to violate its debt-to-equity covenant if corrected. Overall materiality is $250,000. The auditor evaluates the misstatements as quantitatively immaterial. The auditor should:', '["Conclude the misstatements are immaterial based on the quantitative assessment alone", "Disregard the covenant impact because it is a contractual matter outside the financial statements", "Evaluate whether the misstatements'' effect on covenant compliance makes them qualitatively material to financial statement users", "Require management to renegotiate the debt covenant before the report is issued"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A19, the auditor considers qualitative factors including whether a misstatement affects compliance with loan covenants or other contractual requirements. Even though $120,000 is below the $250,000 materiality threshold, the potential covenant violation could trigger significant consequences such as loan acceleration. Relying solely on quantitative analysis (choice A) would be insufficient because AU-C 450 requires evaluation of both quantitative and qualitative aspects.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Tolerable misstatement in sampling context
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is tolerable misstatement in the context of audit sampling under AU-C 530?', '["The maximum misstatement in a population that the auditor can accept and still conclude the audit objective is achieved", "The total amount of misstatements actually identified in the tested sample", "The threshold below which all identified misstatements are considered clearly trivial", "The projected misstatement extrapolated from sample errors to the full population"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 530.05 defines tolerable misstatement as a monetary amount set by the auditor in respect of which the auditor seeks to obtain an appropriate level of assurance that the actual misstatement in the population does not exceed that amount. It is the application of performance materiality to a particular sampling procedure. The total sample misstatement (choice B) is the actual error found, not the acceptable threshold established before testing begins.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Recurring nature of classification misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the current-year audit of Falcon Industries, the auditor discovers a $40,000 classification error between operating and non-operating expenses—the same type of error found and left uncorrected in each of the prior two years. Overall materiality is $300,000. Under AU-C 450, the recurring nature of this misstatement is significant because:', '["Recurring misstatements automatically become material regardless of the dollar amount involved", "The auditor must withdraw from the engagement after three consecutive years of the same error type", "Classification errors are always immaterial since they do not affect the entity''s reported net income", "It indicates a systemic control issue that may warrant reclassification as a significant deficiency or material weakness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.A19, the auditor considers whether a misstatement results from a pattern indicating a possible systematic cause. A recurring classification error across three years suggests an underlying control deficiency that management has not addressed, potentially elevating its significance under AU-C 265. Assuming automatic materiality (choice A) is incorrect—materiality is based on quantitative and qualitative evaluation, not solely on recurrence, though recurrence is an important qualitative factor.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Gross versus net approach to misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Apex Construction, the auditor identifies $90,000 in revenue overstatements and $85,000 in expense overstatements in the same period. Overall materiality is $100,000. Management argues the $5,000 net income effect is immaterial. The auditor should evaluate these misstatements by considering:', '["Only the $5,000 net effect on income since that is the bottom-line impact on users", "Each misstatement only if both were caused by the same underlying control failure", "Each financial statement line item individually, recognizing that both revenue and expenses may be materially misstated even though net income is not", "Neither amount because the offsetting nature eliminates any meaningful misstatement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.11 and .A17, the auditor evaluates misstatements in relation to particular classes of transactions, account balances, and disclosures—not just the net effect on income. Revenue overstated by $90,000 and expenses overstated by $85,000 may each be material to their respective line items. Netting them to $5,000 (choice A) could mask material misstatements in individual financial statement components that users rely on for decision-making.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Communication timing for significant deficiencies and material weaknesses
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, when must the auditor communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance?', '["Within 30 calendar days of completing audit fieldwork", "Immediately upon identification during the course of the audit engagement", "No later than 60 days following the report release date", "Before the entity files its annual tax return with the Internal Revenue Service"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AU-C 265.13 requires the auditor to communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance no later than 60 days following the report release date. This allows time for proper evaluation and drafting of the formal communication. Immediate communication upon identification (choice B) describes interim oral communication, which is encouraged for urgent matters but is not the deadline for the formal written communication.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Group audit misstatement aggregation across components
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The group engagement team for Titan Global Corporation receives summaries of uncorrected misstatements from component auditors in four countries: Component A reports $60,000, Component B reports $45,000, Component C reports $30,000, and Component D reports $25,000. Group materiality is $200,000. The group auditor should:', '["Evaluate each component''s misstatements only against that component''s allocated materiality", "Aggregate all component misstatements and evaluate the total solely against group materiality", "Disregard component misstatements that are individually immaterial at the group level", "Aggregate all component misstatements, evaluate both the total and individual effects on group financial statements, and consider qualitative factors"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 600.40 and AU-C 450.11, the group engagement team aggregates uncorrected misstatements from all components and evaluates their combined effect on the group financial statements. The $160,000 aggregate approaches the $200,000 group materiality and requires evaluation of both quantitative and qualitative factors. Evaluating only at the component level (choice A) would fail to capture the cumulative risk that emerges across the group as a whole.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Deficiency in operation definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, what characterizes a deficiency in the operation of an internal control?', '["The control is missing entirely from the entity''s system of internal control", "A properly designed control does not operate effectively as intended", "The control was originally designed for a different business process or objective", "Management intentionally decided not to implement the control at the entity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 265.07 defines a deficiency in operation as existing when a properly designed control does not operate as designed, or when the person performing the control does not possess the necessary authority or competence. A missing control (choice A) describes a deficiency in design rather than operation. The distinction is important because the remediation approach differs significantly for design versus operational deficiencies.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Severity and likelihood combined evaluation with no compensating controls
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Lakeside Manufacturing, the auditor identifies a control deficiency in the payroll approval process. While no actual misstatement has occurred, payroll constitutes 60% of total expenses and the identified control is the sole safeguard against unauthorized payments. Performance materiality is $150,000. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should evaluate severity based on:', '["The actual misstatement of zero, concluding the deficiency is inconsequential to the audit", "The maximum potential misstatement that could result, regardless of actual occurrence", "Only the likelihood of a misstatement occurring within the next fiscal year", "Both the magnitude of the potential misstatement and the reasonable possibility of occurrence, considering the absence of redundant controls"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A9-A10, severity depends on the magnitude of the potential misstatement and the reasonable possibility that the deficiency could result in misstatement. With payroll at 60% of expenses and no compensating controls, both magnitude and likelihood are elevated despite no actual error. Relying on actual misstatement history alone (choice A) ignores that a material weakness can exist even when no misstatement has yet been detected.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Omitted disclosure as a misstatement
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Bayshore Real Estate, the auditor discovers that a required disclosure about a $3.5 million contingent liability from pending litigation was entirely omitted from the financial statement footnotes. All monetary balances on the face of the statements are fairly stated. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should treat this omission as:', '["A misstatement that must be evaluated for materiality the same as any monetary misstatement in the financial statements", "An immaterial item since no balance sheet or income statement line is numerically misstated", "A disclosure deficiency outside the scope of the auditor''s misstatement evaluation process", "A significant deficiency in internal control but not a financial statement misstatement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.04, misstatements include omissions of required disclosures, not just errors in monetary amounts on the face of financial statements. The omission of a $3.5 million contingent liability disclosure must be evaluated for materiality just as any other misstatement. Treating it as outside misstatement evaluation (choice C) is incorrect because AU-C 450 explicitly encompasses qualitative disclosures and omitted information that could influence users'' economic decisions.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Fraud-related misstatement implications for control deficiency classification
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Golden Arch Retailers, the auditor discovers that a store manager fabricated $22,000 in fictitious vendor invoices over six months, circumventing the three-way match control because a supervisor rubber-stamped approvals. Overall materiality is $400,000. Under AU-C 265 and AU-C 240, the auditor should:', '["Classify the deficiency as inconsequential because the actual loss is well below materiality", "Consider the fraud a significant deficiency only if the manager held a senior financial role", "Defer evaluation until the fraud investigation is complete and all final losses are quantified", "Evaluate whether the control failure that allowed the fraud constitutes a material weakness regardless of the dollar amount"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, fraud committed by employees in a position to circumvent controls is a strong indicator of a material weakness, regardless of the dollar amount involved. The supervisor''s failure to properly review invoices allowed the fraud to continue undetected for six months. Dismissing it based on the dollar amount (choice A) ignores that AU-C 265 considers both actual and potential magnitude—the same deficiency could have resulted in much larger losses.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — Purpose of accumulating misstatements during the audit
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Aspen Valley Publishing has $12,000 in uncorrected misstatements from revenue testing, $8,000 from inventory testing, and $5,000 from payroll testing. Overall materiality is $50,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor accumulates these individual misstatements primarily to:', '["Determine whether additional audit procedures are needed for each separate account area", "Evaluate whether the aggregate of $25,000 approaches or exceeds materiality for the financial statements as a whole", "Calculate the penalty amount that management must remit to external regulators", "Establish next year''s planning materiality allocation for each individual account"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.10-11, the auditor accumulates all identified misstatements other than those clearly trivial and evaluates whether the aggregate is material to the financial statements as a whole. The $25,000 combined total is assessed against the $50,000 materiality threshold. Evaluating each area separately (choice A) would miss the cumulative effect that AU-C 450 specifically requires the auditor to consider across all areas tested during the engagement.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Recipients of material weakness communications
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, to whom must the auditor communicate material weaknesses identified during a financial statement audit?', '["Management and those charged with governance, in writing", "Only the external financial statement users through the audit report", "The Securities and Exchange Commission within 30 days of discovery", "Only the entity''s internal audit department for corrective action"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 265.11 requires the auditor to communicate material weaknesses in writing to both management and those charged with governance. This dual communication ensures that those responsible for both operations and oversight are informed. Communicating only to external users (choice B) is incorrect because the communication is directed to parties within the entity who can evaluate conditions and take corrective action.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Known (factual) misstatement definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is a known misstatement as described in auditing standards?', '["A misstatement about which there is no doubt because it has been specifically identified through audit procedures", "A misstatement that management has acknowledged but formally declined to correct", "An error the auditor expects to find based on prior-year audit experience with the client", "A projected error extrapolated from a sample of transactions to the full population"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450, a known misstatement (also called a factual misstatement) is one about which there is no doubt—it has been specifically identified through audit procedures. Examples include a specific unrecorded invoice or a calculation error in a schedule. A projected misstatement (choice D) is fundamentally different—it represents the auditor''s best estimate of misstatements in populations based on extrapolation from sample results.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Significant deficiency threshold standard
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, how is a significant deficiency in internal control defined?', '["A deficiency that has caused an actual material misstatement in the current period", "A deficiency that management considers important enough to disclose publicly", "A deficiency identical in severity to a material weakness requiring report modification", "A deficiency or combination of deficiencies that is less severe than a material weakness yet important enough to merit attention by those charged with governance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'AU-C 265.07 defines a significant deficiency as a deficiency, or combination of deficiencies, in internal control that is less severe than a material weakness yet important enough to merit attention by those charged with governance. This standard focuses on governance-level importance rather than actual losses. Whether actual losses occurred (choice A) is not determinative—a significant deficiency can exist even when no misstatement has resulted from it.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Compensating control effect on deficiency severity evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Heritage Financial Corp, the auditor identifies that the accounts payable clerk can both enter and approve invoices without system-enforced segregation of duties. However, the CFO performs a detailed monthly review of all vendor payments exceeding $1,000, which has caught errors in the past. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should:', '["Automatically classify the segregation issue as a material weakness regardless of other controls", "Ignore the segregation deficiency entirely since the compensating control has proven effective", "Evaluate whether the CFO''s compensating review sufficiently reduces the severity of the segregation deficiency", "Require the entity to implement system-enforced segregation before issuing the audit report"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A7, the auditor considers the existence of compensating controls when evaluating the severity of a deficiency. The CFO''s detailed monthly review may reduce the risk that the segregation issue leads to material misstatement. However, the auditor must evaluate whether the compensating control''s design, frequency, and effectiveness adequately mitigate the risk. Ignoring the deficiency entirely (choice B) is incorrect because compensating controls reduce severity but may not fully eliminate it.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Management override as indicator of material weakness
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Silverton Manufacturing, the auditor discovers that the CEO instructed accounting staff to record $150,000 in revenue for a shipment that did not occur until January of the following year. No other controls prevented this entry. Overall materiality is $750,000. Under AU-C 265, management override of controls to record fictitious revenue is best characterized as:', '["An inherent limitation of internal control that requires no further evaluation by the auditor", "A significant deficiency because the dollar amount is below the overall materiality threshold", "A material weakness because management override to commit fraud is a strong indicator regardless of the dollar amount", "A deficiency in the design of the revenue recognition control only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A12 and AU-C 240, identified fraud involving senior management—including override of controls—is a strong indicator of a material weakness in internal control. The intentional manipulation of revenue demonstrates that controls cannot prevent management-directed misstatement. Classifying it as merely a significant deficiency (choice B) understates the severity because fraud by senior management transcends quantitative materiality thresholds in evaluating control deficiency severity.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L3 — PCAOB versus AICPA deficiency communication requirements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'A CPA firm audits both private companies under AICPA standards and public companies under PCAOB standards. The engagement partner asks the audit manager to compare the deficiency communication requirements. Under PCAOB AS 2201 for issuer audits versus AU-C 265 for nonissuer audits, a key difference is that:', '["PCAOB standards require the auditor to express an opinion on internal control effectiveness, while AU-C 265 requires only written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses", "PCAOB standards do not require communication of material weaknesses to those charged with governance", "AICPA standards require auditors to communicate all control deficiencies to shareholders", "Both sets of standards have identical communication and reporting requirements for deficiencies"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under PCAOB AS 2201, auditors of issuers must express an opinion on the effectiveness of internal control over financial reporting as part of an integrated audit, which goes beyond the AU-C 265 requirement to communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing. The PCAOB''s integrated audit mandate creates a higher reporting threshold for public companies. Claiming identical requirements (choice D) ignores this fundamental distinction between issuer and nonissuer audit standards.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Turnaround journal entries and misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pacific Coast Distributors, the auditor identifies that management recorded a $60,000 year-end accrual with a reversing entry in January. Investigation reveals the accrual lacked any underlying obligation and was recorded to meet fourth-quarter earnings targets. Overall materiality is $250,000. The auditor should treat this turnaround entry as:', '["An acceptable accounting practice since reversing entries are a routine part of the close process", "A judgmental misstatement that should simply be accumulated with other uncorrected items", "An immaterial timing difference that will self-correct in the subsequent reporting period", "An intentional misstatement indicating possible fraudulent financial reporting under AU-C 240"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 240.05 and AU-C 450, a deliberate accrual without economic substance recorded to meet earnings targets constitutes an intentional misstatement indicative of fraud, not a legitimate timing difference. The turnaround nature does not diminish its significance. Treating it as routine (choice A) ignores the fraudulent intent—AU-C 240 requires the auditor to consider conditions indicating manipulation of results through journal entries lacking business substance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Auditor response when aggregate approaches materiality
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Riverview Technologies, the auditor accumulates $180,000 in uncorrected misstatements against an overall materiality of $200,000. Management declines to make corrections, asserting the misstatements are immaterial. Under AU-C 450, when the aggregate closely approaches materiality, the auditor should:', '["Accept management''s assessment and issue an unmodified opinion without further action", "Request management to correct the misstatements, consider performing additional procedures to reduce detection risk, and evaluate whether the opinion should be modified", "Automatically issue an adverse opinion because the aggregate is within 10% of materiality", "Reduce overall materiality retroactively to ensure the aggregate exceeds the revised threshold"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.12-13, when the aggregate of uncorrected misstatements approaches materiality, the auditor recognizes a greater risk that undetected misstatements could push the total above materiality. The auditor should request corrections and may perform additional procedures to reduce this risk. Automatically issuing an adverse opinion (choice C) is incorrect—the auditor first exhausts options to reduce the risk, and a qualified opinion rather than adverse would typically result if the aggregate remains material.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Individually immaterial misstatements exceeding materiality in aggregate
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Continental Supply Group, the auditor identifies eight individually immaterial misstatements across different accounts, each ranging from $10,000 to $18,000. Overall materiality is $100,000 and the aggregate total of all eight misstatements is $105,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Evaluate the aggregate as a material uncorrected misstatement and determine the effect on the auditor''s opinion", "Dismiss all eight misstatements because each one is individually immaterial to users", "Report each misstatement individually to those charged with governance without performing any aggregation", "Request management to correct only the three largest items to bring the aggregate below the materiality threshold"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor evaluates uncorrected misstatements individually and in aggregate. Although each misstatement is individually immaterial, the $105,000 aggregate exceeds the $100,000 materiality threshold, making the financial statements materially misstated as a whole. Dismissing them because each is individually immaterial (choice B) violates the aggregation requirement that is fundamental to AU-C 450''s framework for protecting financial statement users from cumulative errors.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
